<div class="titlepage"><div><div><h2 class="title" style="clear: both">
<a name="zend.pdf.save"></a>55.3. Save Changes to PDF Documents</h2></div></div></div>
<p>
        There are two methods that save changes to <acronym class="acronym">PDF</acronym> documents: the
        <code class="methodname">Zend_Pdf::save()</code> and <code class="methodname">Zend_Pdf::render()</code>
        methods.
    </p>
<p>
        <code class="methodname">Zend_Pdf::save($filename, $updateOnly = false)</code> saves the
        <acronym class="acronym">PDF</acronym> document to a file. If $updateOnly is <code class="constant">TRUE</code>,
        then only the new <acronym class="acronym">PDF</acronym> file segment is appended to a file. Otherwise, the
        file is overwritten.
    </p>
<p>
        <code class="methodname">Zend_Pdf::render($newSegmentOnly = false)</code> returns the
        <acronym class="acronym">PDF</acronym> document as a string. If $newSegmentOnly is
        <code class="constant">TRUE</code>, then only the new <acronym class="acronym">PDF</acronym> file segment is
        returned.
    </p>
<div class="example">
<a name="zend.pdf.save.example-1"></a><p class="title"><b>Example 55.3. Saving PDF Documents</b></p>
<div class="example-contents"><pre class="programlisting">
...
// Load the PDF document
$pdf = Zend_Pdf::load($fileName);
...
// Update the PDF document
$pdf-&gt;save($fileName, true);
// Save document as a new file
$pdf-&gt;save($newFileName);

// Return the PDF document as a string
$pdfString = $pdf-&gt;render();

...
</pre></div>
</div>
<br class="example-break">
</div>
